## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design end-to-end, scalable data ingestion with evolving schemas and strict access controls.

## Key Concepts
- Schema evolution and registry in GCS
- Event-driven ingest via Pub/Sub and Dataflow
- Idempotent writes to BigQuery and quarantined handling
- Schema drift detection and monitoring
- IAM minimal privileges and secure service-to-service communication

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator: fetch schema from registry, validate event, route accordingly
async function ingest(event) {
  const schema = await fetchSchema(event.tenant);
  if (validate(event, schema)) {
    writeBigQuery(event);
  } else {
    routeQuarantine(event);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement schema registry versioning and backward-compatibility rules?
- How would you test idempotence and drift detection at scale?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests end-to-end data ingestion, validation, redaction, and end-of-day analytics using beginner-friendly GCP primitives, plus a focus on security and idempotency.

## Key Concepts

- Cloud Run HTTPS endpoints
- Pub/Sub integration
- Cloud Functions for validation and redaction
- Cloud Storage with date-based prefixes
- BigQuery nightly ETL
- Idempotency via insertId
- Minimal IAM bindings and testability

## Code Example

